Component Summary
Component	Purpose
Resource Group	Groups related Azure resources together
Databricks Workspace	Development and execution environment for Spark workloads
Storage Account (ADLS Gen2)	Stores data files and tables
Container (raw)	Top-level storage location for files
Access Connector	Managed Identity used by Databricks to authenticate with Azure
IAM Role (Storage Blob Data Contributor)	Grants the Access Connector permission to read/write blobs
Storage Credential	Unity Catalog object that defines how Databricks authenticates to storage
External Location	Unity Catalog object that maps a storage path to a storage credential
Notebook	Runs Spark code to read and process data
Production Best Practices

In a production data platform, you'll typically organize storage into layers such as:

ADLS Gen2
│
├── raw/
│   └── Source data as received
│
├── bronze/
│   └── Minimal transformations
│
├── silver/
│   └── Cleaned and validated data
│
├── gold/
│   └── Business-ready, aggregated datasets
│
└── archive/
    └── Historical backups and old files

Databricks notebooks read from raw, transform the data through the Bronze → Silver → Gold layers, and Unity Catalog governs secure access throughout the process.

This structure not only documents how to build the environment but also why each component exists, which makes it valuable for onboarding, interviews, and real-world reference.